CAT = "gmdGiRenalEndo"; SEG = "GRE"; POOL = 16
Q = [
("Apart from H. pylori, what is the leading cause of peptic ulcer disease?",
 "NSAID use", ["Spicy food", "Work stress", "Coffee intake"],
 "H. pylori infection and NSAIDs, including low-dose aspirin, cause most peptic ulcers. Diet, coffee and everyday stress do not cause ulcers on their own."),
("Which drug is first-line to induce remission in mild to moderate ulcerative colitis?",
 "Mesalazine (5-ASA)", ["Azathioprine", "Infliximab", "Ciclosporin"],
 "ECCO guidance starts oral plus rectal 5-aminosalicylate (mesalazine) for mild to moderate ulcerative colitis. Thiopurines maintain remission, and biologics and ciclosporin are for moderate to severe or refractory disease."),
("A patient has a positive hepatitis C antibody test. Which test confirms current infection?",
 "HCV RNA", ["Repeat antibody test", "Serum ALT level", "Liver biopsy"],
 "WHO hepatitis C testing guidance confirms viremia with a nucleic acid test for HCV RNA (or core antigen) after a reactive antibody test. Antibodies stay positive after cleared infection."),
("A patient with cirrhosis and ascites has fever. Which ascitic result confirms spontaneous bacterial peritonitis?",
 "Neutrophils over 250/mm3", ["Lymphocytes over 1,000/mm3", "Total protein over 25 g/L", "SAAG over 11 g/L"],
 "An ascitic neutrophil count above 250 cells/mm3 diagnoses SBP and starts antibiotics. A SAAG of 11 g/L or more shows portal hypertension, not infection."),
("Besides antibiotics, which drug is started at once for suspected variceal bleeding?",
 "Terlipressin", ["Propranolol", "Tranexamic acid", "Vitamin K"],
 "Baveno guidance gives a vasoactive drug (terlipressin, somatostatin or octreotide) and antibiotic prophylaxis before endoscopy. Propranolol is for prevention once bleeding is controlled; tranexamic acid did not help in GI bleeding."),
("Which itchy blistering rash on the elbows is linked with celiac disease?",
 "Dermatitis herpetiformis", ["Erythema nodosum on shins", "Pyoderma gangrenosum", "Acanthosis nigricans"],
 "Dermatitis herpetiformis is the skin form of gluten sensitivity and clears on a gluten-free diet. Erythema nodosum and pyoderma gangrenosum are linked with inflammatory bowel disease."),
("A patient with heartburn has which alarm feature that calls for prompt endoscopy?",
 "Progressive dysphagia", ["Pain after large meals", "Relief with antacid tablets", "Night-time symptoms"],
 "Dysphagia, weight loss, GI bleeding, anemia and persistent vomiting are alarm features that need endoscopy. Symptoms after meals or at night that respond to antacids fit uncomplicated reflux."),
("Which ECG change suggests hypocalcemia?",
 "Prolonged QT interval", ["Short QT interval", "Peaked T waves", "Delta waves"],
 "Low calcium lengthens the ST segment and so the QT interval. High calcium shortens the QT; peaked T waves point to hyperkalemia."),
("What is the main danger of lowering chronic hypernatremia too quickly?",
 "Cerebral edema", ["Osmotic demyelination", "Rebound hypokalemia", "Acute pancreatitis"],
 "Brain cells adapt to chronic hypernatremia by holding solutes, so a fast fall in sodium draws water in and causes cerebral edema. Osmotic demyelination is the risk of raising low sodium too fast."),
("Under KDIGO, which creatinine change defines acute kidney injury?",
 "Rise of 0.3 mg/dL in 48 hours", ["Rise of 0.3 mg/dL in 7 days", "Rise to 1.2 times baseline in 7 days", "eGFR below 60 for 3 months"],
 "KDIGO defines AKI as a creatinine rise of 0.3 mg/dL or more within 48 hours, a rise to 1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or low urine output. An eGFR below 60 for 3 months is chronic kidney disease."),
("In type 2 diabetes with albuminuria on full renin-angiotensin blockade, which added drug class slows kidney decline?",
 "SGLT2 inhibitor", ["Sulfonylurea", "Thiazolidinedione", "Loop diuretic"],
 "The KDIGO 2022 diabetes and CKD guideline recommends an SGLT2 inhibitor for most patients with eGFR of 20 or more, as trials showed slower loss of kidney function."),
("Which clue points to renovascular hypertension?",
 "Creatinine jump on ACE inhibitor", ["Low potassium off diuretics", "Episodes of headache and sweating", "Weak femoral pulses"],
 "A creatinine rise of over 30% after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis. Low potassium points to aldosteronism, spells to pheochromocytoma and weak femoral pulses to coarctation."),
("A raised TSH with a normal free T4 is called:",
 "Subclinical hypothyroidism", ["Central (secondary) hypothyroidism", "Sick euthyroid state", "Subclinical hyperthyroidism"],
 "Subclinical hypothyroidism is a high TSH with free T4 in range. Central hypothyroidism gives a low or normal TSH with low free T4."),
("Which fasting plasma glucose meets the ADA threshold for diabetes?",
 "126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L)", ["100 mg/dL (5.6 mmol/L)", "110 mg/dL (6.1 mmol/L)", "200 mg/dL (11.1 mmol/L)"],
 "ADA Standards of Care diagnose diabetes at a fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or more, confirmed by repeat testing. 100 to 125 mg/dL is prediabetes, and 200 mg/dL is the random or 2-hour threshold."),
("In a new adult case of diabetes, which test best supports autoimmune type 1 disease?",
 "GAD antibodies", ["HbA1c level", "Fasting lipid profile", "Glucose tolerance test"],
 "Islet autoantibodies such as GAD65 confirm autoimmune diabetes, including latent autoimmune diabetes in adults; a low C-peptide supports it. HbA1c and glucose tests diagnose diabetes but not its type."),
("Which drug is given at once for suspected adrenal crisis?",
 "IV hydrocortisone", ["Oral fludrocortisone", "IV levothyroxine bolus", "IV insulin"],
 "Endocrine Society guidance gives hydrocortisone 100 mg IV or IM at once, with saline, without waiting for test results. Fludrocortisone is for long-term mineralocorticoid replacement."),
]
